# What must never reach the image.
#
# `COPY . .` takes the working tree as it stands, so anything sitting beside the
# source ships inside the image and stays in its layers. Without this file the
# image contained the live .env — Cloudflare token, database password and
# SECRET_KEY — the development database, and the unredacted scan reports. None
# of that is recoverable by deleting the files in a later layer: the earlier
# layer still holds them, and anyone who pulls the image can read it.
#
# It is also why builds were flaky and slow. The context was 206 MB, 193 MB of
# it a Windows virtualenv, checksummed over a 9p mount on every build.

# Secrets. The container gets its configuration from the environment at run
# time; the file itself has no business being inside.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
config/caddy/certs/
config/caddy/dns/

# A host virtualenv is the wrong platform and the wrong Python.
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Local state. The container mounts volumes for these.
data/
staticfiles/
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite3-*

# The originals behind the scan-report corpus: real VINs, real vehicles. The
# redacted .words.json captures beside them are safe and stay, so a test run
# inside the container still has a corpus to work with.
#
# These rules mirror .gitignore's, and that is the point rather than a
# coincidence: what must not reach a public repository and what must not reach
# a published image are the same set. The two lists drifting apart is how 349 MB
# of untracked research and five photographs of our own vehicles came to be
# inside a locally built image. Change one, change the other.
#
# Excluding `Artifacts/samples/` wholesale is the obvious-looking version, and
# it is wrong. Every corpus test skips politely when the corpus is absent
# (scantools/fixtures.py says so, having been caught by it once), so the suite
# would go green in the container having read none of the 343 captures. Keep
# the captures; drop what produced them.
Artifacts/samples/**/*.pdf
# `**/`, not `*/` — the same bug .gitignore carries a comment about, which was
# fixed there and never mirrored here. This line read `Artifacts/samples/*.jpg`
# while its three neighbours matched at any depth, so the BT600 Plus printouts
# one folder further down were photographed, gitignored, and then shipped.
Artifacts/samples/**/*.jpg
Artifacts/samples/**/*.jpeg
Artifacts/samples/**/*.png
Artifacts/samples/backups/
# Somebody else's reports, kept for their formats, and the manifests whose URLs
# carry the VIN the report was made for. Not ours to redistribute — in a public
# repository or in a published image. 333 MB of the two.
Artifacts/samples/**/originals/
Artifacts/samples/**/automotive_scan_tool_public_examples*.json
# Source documents for the transcribed code lists, and LMC Truck's published
# chassis-identification sheets. Both are cited by what was read out of them and
# read by nothing: what ships is the transcription, which names its source.
Artifacts/samples/dtc-lists/
Artifacts/VIN Decoding/
Data/

# History, tooling and editor noise.
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.idea/
.vscode/
# `**/`, not bare `*.md`, for the same reason as the .jpg line above: a
# dockerignore `*` does not cross a `/`, so this rule read `*.md` and excluded
# the four at the root while shipping SPEC.md, REFERENCE.md, both integration
# contracts and four stray READMEs — about a megabyte of design documents in
# every image, none of which anything reads at runtime. The repository is where
# those are read; `org.opencontainers.image.source` on the image points at it.
**/*.md
!README.md
docs/
locale/*/LC_MESSAGES/*.po

# Compose builds the image; the image does not need to describe itself.
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
